References to two unreleased controllers that identify as Apple hardware in code were <a href="https://forums.macrumors.com/threads/apple-is-working-on-two-unreleased-game-controllers.2488482/">spotted in the macOS 26.7 release candidate</a> by <em>MacRumors</em> reader Pdfu. Apple's first macOS 26.7 release candidate came out in mid-August, and it was <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/08/17/macos-26-7-unreleased-apple-devices/">full of references</a> to unreleased Apple devices, from iPhones and Macs to new home accessories. Pdfu has continued to dig into the RC, finding new details that were previously overlooked.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2024/10/apple-game-controller-graphic.jpg.png" alt="" width="1600" height="900" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-969801" /><br/>
There are GameController profiles for two hardware devices identified as T6502 and T1057, which Pdfu suggests could be Apple gaming controllers in development. The two controllers have different haptic designs and standard controller components, like a D-pad, dual thumbsticks, shoulder buttons, Home and Menu buttons, and analog triggers. T1057 also has accelerometer and gyroscope support, while T6502 doesn't.<br/>
<br/>
The code includes dedicated hardware plug-ins for each device as well, which suggests these are actual devices instead of placeholder code.<br/>
<br/>
There have been rumors about a <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2012/04/02/apple-has-worked-on-a-physical-game-controller-for-iphone/">possible Apple game controller</a> over the years, but nothing has ever materialized.<br/>
<br/>
The code found in the first <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macos-26/">macOS Tahoe</a> 26.7 RC could point to an Apple controller in the works, but it could also be referencing internal test hardware Apple uses for testing game controller code and support. Apple removed the code in the second &zwnj;macOS Tahoe&zwnj; 26.7 RC.<br/>
<br/>
Apple has had multiple opportunities to release a game controller. The company could have launched one alongside any version of the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/apple-tv/">Apple TV</a>, when <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/apple-arcade/">Apple Arcade</a> came out, or when the Vision Pro launched, but it didn't. Instead, Apple focused on building extensive support for third-party controllers.<br/>
<br/>
In 2015, Apple required &zwnj;Apple TV&zwnj; games <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2015/09/15/apple-tv-games-support-apple-tv-remote/">to support the Siri Remote</a> as a gaming controller, but <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2016/06/14/apple-drops-siri-remote-game-controller-demand/">gave that up</a> in a matter of months. With the Vision Pro, it <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/11/12/apple-now-sells-ps-vr2-sense-controllers/">partnered with Sony</a> to add Vision Pro support to the PlayStation VR2 Sense controllers instead of designing its own controller.<br/>
<br/>
Console makers have mastered the controller, and many people already have Xbox, PlayStation, or Nintendo controllers. There's little improvement to be made on controller ergonomics, but if Apple is working on a controller, features like <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/find-my/">Find My</a> and instant cross-device pairing could set it apart from competitors.<br/>
<br/>
